1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:11,993
The Shop, it turned out, had been waiting for
him to own the first piece.

2
00:00:12,563 --> 00:00:18,501
The day after the ten-pull, a new line had appeared
in the blueprint catalogue, greyed

3
00:00:18,521 --> 00:00:24,599
out and unpriced. Cellular Renewal Compound —
fragment two of three. A week later it had

4
00:00:24,619 --> 00:00:30,488
a price. A week after that, fragment three appeared
beside it. Twelve thousand Points

5
00:00:30,508 --> 00:00:30,835
each.

6
00:00:31,525 --> 00:00:33,190
It is baiting you.

7
00:00:33,710 --> 00:00:38,652
It's teaching me. There's a difference. It wants me
to learn that the best things come

8
00:00:38,672 --> 00:00:41,882
in pieces, and that you pay for the
last piece the most.

9
00:00:43,033 --> 00:00:47,314
Blueprint complete. Cellular
renewal compound.

10
00:00:49,453 --> 00:00:53,055
What arrived, when the three pieces
joined, was a medicine.

11
00:00:53,625 --> 00:00:59,372
It was not the Prototype. It was not anything like
the Prototype. The Wesker half of the

12
00:00:59,392 --> 00:01:05,334
mind read it three times to be sure, because the
Wesker half had spent a lifetime designing

13
00:01:05,354 --> 00:01:09,266
things that killed people, and he was
looking for the trick.

14
00:01:09,837 --> 00:01:15,714
There was no trick. The compound was a small, stable
molecule that could be made in any

15
00:01:15,734 --> 00:01:22,017
competent chemical plant on Earth. It found cells in
the human body that had stopped dividing

16
00:01:22,037 --> 00:01:28,118
but refused to die — the old, stubborn, inflamed cells
that accumulate with age and poison

17
00:01:28,138 --> 00:01:34,083
everything around them — and it told them, gently, to
finish dying. And then it told the

18
00:01:34,103 --> 00:01:35,981
cells around them to repair.

19
00:01:36,551 --> 00:01:42,739
In mice, according to the documentation, it extended
healthy lifespan by forty percent.

20
00:01:42,759 --> 00:01:49,018
In the elderly, it would reverse most of the
measurable signs of physical decline within

21
00:01:49,038 --> 00:01:49,518
a year.

22
00:01:51,207 --> 00:01:53,641
You are going to make old men young.

23
00:01:54,162 --> 00:02:00,253
I'm going to make old men grateful. And very rich
old men very, very grateful. Do you

24
00:02:00,273 --> 00:02:05,358
know who runs every country on this planet,
Adolf? Men over sixty-five.

25
00:02:07,376 --> 00:02:13,909
They called it Aeterna. Tomás suggested the name.
Catherine hated it. The Director approved

26
00:02:13,929 --> 00:02:20,462
it in four seconds, because the Adolf half of the mind
had heard it and said, very quietly,

27
00:02:20,482 --> 00:02:20,750
yes.

28
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:27,420
The first human trial began in September of twenty
twenty-seven. Sixty volunteers, all

29
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:33,682
over seventy, all suffering from the slow, grinding
frailty that doctors call sarcopenia

30
00:02:33,702 --> 00:02:40,157
and everyone else calls getting old. It was a narrow,
careful, entirely conventional study,

31
00:02:40,177 --> 00:02:46,348
designed by Nadia Okafor's team and approved by an
independent ethics board, and nobody

32
00:02:46,368 --> 00:02:52,040
involved in it except the man at the top had any
idea what it was going to show.

33
00:02:52,610 --> 00:02:58,434
By December, a seventy-eight-year-old retired
steelworker named Arthur Kowalczyk, who

34
00:02:58,454 --> 00:03:04,622
had arrived at the trial in a wheelchair, walked out
of it. He walked to his car. He drove

35
00:03:04,642 --> 00:03:10,053
home. He called his daughter in Ohio and cried
on the phone for twenty minutes.

36
00:03:10,623 --> 00:03:16,508
By January, the grip strength of the treatment group
had increased by thirty-one percent.

37
00:03:16,528 --> 00:03:22,213
Their walking speed by twenty-six. Their
inflammatory markers had fallen to levels you

38
00:03:22,233 --> 00:03:27,918
would expect in people twenty years younger. Two of
them had had cataracts clear. One,

39
00:03:27,938 --> 00:03:33,557
a woman of eighty-one, had begun — very slightly —
to regrow hair that had been white

40
00:03:33,577 --> 00:03:36,012
for a decade, in its original colour.

41
00:03:37,581 --> 00:03:43,523
Nadia Okafor read the interim data at eleven
o'clock at night, alone in her office on

42
00:03:43,543 --> 00:03:49,485
the eighth floor. She read it twice. Then she took
off her glasses and sat for a long

43
00:03:49,505 --> 00:03:51,168
time looking at nothing.

44
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:57,381
She was a good scientist. She knew what she was
looking at. It was the most important

45
00:03:57,401 --> 00:03:59,378
medical result in fifty years.

46
00:03:59,948 --> 00:04:05,937
And it had come from a compound whose design history
she had tried to trace three times,

47
00:04:05,957 --> 00:04:12,084
and which led, every time, to a folder of documents
that had simply appeared on the server

48
00:04:12,104 --> 00:04:15,977
one morning, fully formed, with no
author she could find.

49
00:04:18,048 --> 00:04:22,201
The data went public in February. The
world lost its mind.

50
00:04:22,769 --> 00:04:28,632
Umbrella's share price went from eighteen dollars to
a hundred and four in nine trading

51
00:04:28,652 --> 00:04:34,380
days. The Food and Drug Administration, under an
administration that had spent a year

52
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:40,128
promising to cut red tape for American medicine,
granted Aeterna breakthrough therapy

53
00:04:40,148 --> 00:04:46,281
designation in a record thirty-one days. Every health
desk on Earth ran the story of Arthur

54
00:04:46,301 --> 00:04:48,242
Kowalczyk walking to his car.

55
00:04:48,814 --> 00:04:53,615
And every television producer on Earth wanted
the man in the sunglasses.

56
00:04:55,305 --> 00:05:00,458
No television. We're a clinical-stage biotech with
one trial and sixty patients. Every

57
00:05:00,478 --> 00:05:05,692
word he says on camera is a word the SEC can read
back to us. We do a press release, we

58
00:05:05,712 --> 00:05:08,338
do an investor call, and we do
nothing else.

59
00:05:08,858 --> 00:05:13,402
With respect, Catherine, that's how you do it if
you want to be a biotech. He doesn't

60
00:05:13,422 --> 00:05:14,530
want to be a biotech.

61
00:05:15,050 --> 00:05:16,481
What does he want to be?

62
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:21,554
I don't know yet. But I know what the internet wants
him to be. There are three hundred

63
00:05:21,574 --> 00:05:25,655
thousand people in a subreddit right now arguing
about whether he's a vampire.

64
00:05:26,975 --> 00:05:27,905
One interview.

65
00:05:28,425 --> 00:05:29,257
Albert —

66
00:05:29,776 --> 00:05:35,548
One. Live. Your conditions on what I can say.
Tomás's conditions on everything else.

67
00:05:37,569 --> 00:05:43,128
The interview was on a Wednesday morning, on the
flagship programme of a business network

68
00:05:43,148 --> 00:05:46,200
that forty million people watched
over breakfast.

69
00:05:46,769 --> 00:05:52,455
The anchor was a woman named Miriam Castell, who
had interviewed three presidents and

70
00:05:52,475 --> 00:05:58,228
most of the world's billionaires, and who had a
reputation for making powerful men say

71
00:05:58,248 --> 00:06:03,062
one sentence too many. She had prepared for
four days. She had a folder.

72
00:06:03,632 --> 00:06:09,580
He arrived four minutes before air, which gave the
makeup department time to do nothing,

73
00:06:09,600 --> 00:06:14,259
and sat down across from her in a black
suit, with his sunglasses on.

74
00:06:14,949 --> 00:06:17,199
The glasses. Would you mind? For
the audience.

75
00:06:17,720 --> 00:06:19,087
The lights are very bright.

76
00:06:19,605 --> 00:06:21,028
I'm afraid it looks a little —

77
00:06:21,548 --> 00:06:22,745
I know how it looks.

78
00:06:24,064 --> 00:06:29,578
She let it go. She would say afterwards that it was
the first time in twenty years she

79
00:06:29,598 --> 00:06:33,890
had let anything go before air, and she
never quite worked out why.

80
00:06:35,461 --> 00:06:36,907
The red light came on.

81
00:06:37,475 --> 00:06:43,482
The first eight minutes were ordinary. She asked about
the trial, and he answered, briefly

82
00:06:43,502 --> 00:06:49,174
and precisely, in sentences Catherine had approved.
She asked about Verdant Axis, and

83
00:06:49,194 --> 00:06:55,334
he said that the company's scientists had done
excellent work under difficult circumstances.

84
00:06:55,354 --> 00:07:01,361
She asked about the Shenzhen licence, and he said that
science did not have a nationality,

85
00:07:01,381 --> 00:07:07,655
and that he was an American company's chief executive,
and that he paid his taxes in Delaware,

86
00:07:07,675 --> 00:07:11,003
which was more than some of his
critics could say.

87
00:07:11,574 --> 00:07:17,303
And then, at eight minutes and ten seconds, Miriam
Castell leaned forward and asked the

88
00:07:17,323 --> 00:07:20,673
question she had built the entire
interview around.

89
00:07:21,963 --> 00:07:26,514
Doctor Wesker, people are calling Aeterna a fountain
of youth. They're saying you've found

90
00:07:26,534 --> 00:07:30,120
a way to stop ageing. I have to ask you
directly. Is that what this is?

91
00:07:31,639 --> 00:07:33,040
He did not answer.

92
00:07:36,108 --> 00:07:42,596
He did not look away. He did not smile. He simply sat,
perfectly still, with the red light

93
00:07:42,615 --> 00:07:47,656
of the camera in the lenses of his glasses,
and let the silence go on.

94
00:07:50,726 --> 00:07:55,947
In the control room, the director began to count
under his breath. Four. Five. Six. In

95
00:07:55,967 --> 00:08:01,126
a live broadcast, three seconds of silence is an
accident. Six is a crisis. At eight,

96
00:08:01,146 --> 00:08:06,610
a producer reached for the button that would cut to a
commercial, and the director slapped

97
00:08:06,630 --> 00:08:11,972
her hand away, because he had been in television for
twenty-six years, and he knew — the

98
00:08:11,992 --> 00:08:17,394
way you know a storm is coming — that he was watching
something he would never see again.

99
00:08:19,964 --> 00:08:21,783
Nine. Ten. Seventeen.

100
00:08:23,673 --> 00:08:29,151
No, Miss Castell. It isn't a fountain of youth. A
fountain is something you stumble on

101
00:08:29,171 --> 00:08:31,069
in the forest. This was built.

102
00:08:32,589 --> 00:08:38,356
That was all. Seventeen words, after eleven seconds
of nothing. Miriam Castell, who had

103
00:08:38,376 --> 00:08:44,011
made three presidents say one sentence too many,
found she had nothing to follow them

104
00:08:44,031 --> 00:08:48,135
with. She thanked him. He thanked her.
The red light went off.

105
00:08:50,206 --> 00:08:55,668
The clip was online in four minutes. By noon it
had been watched ten million times.

106
00:08:56,238 --> 00:09:01,877
By the evening, it had a name. Somebody on the
internet had called him the Sunglasses

107
00:09:01,897 --> 00:09:07,603
CEO, and the name had stuck the way names stick
online — instantly, and forever. There

108
00:09:07,623 --> 00:09:13,663
were edits of the eleven seconds set to orchestral
music. There were edits set to heartbeat

109
00:09:13,683 --> 00:09:19,522
sounds. There was an edit in which someone had slowed
the silence down to a full minute,

110
00:09:19,542 --> 00:09:25,381
and it had been watched four million times on its
own. There were people who counted the

111
00:09:25,401 --> 00:09:31,108
seconds out loud in their videos, in the style of a
rocket launch. There were T-shirts

112
00:09:31,128 --> 00:09:31,774
by Friday.

113
00:09:32,344 --> 00:09:37,839
Tomás watched it happen from a room on the tenth
floor with six screens and a budget,

114
00:09:37,859 --> 00:09:40,046
and he did not sleep for two days.

115
00:09:40,615 --> 00:09:46,319
It was Tomás who found the break room clip — the
twenty seconds from November, the room

116
00:09:46,339 --> 00:09:52,175
nobody was watching — and released it, the following
Tuesday, with a single line of text:

117
00:09:52,195 --> 00:09:58,032
where it started. It was Tomás who made sure that
every edit of the eleven seconds linked

118
00:09:58,052 --> 00:10:03,691
back to the Aeterna trial page. It was Tomás who
understood, before anyone else in the

119
00:10:03,711 --> 00:10:07,705
company, that the Director was not a
chief executive anymore.

120
00:10:08,275 --> 00:10:13,920
He was a story. And stories, on the internet of
twenty twenty-eight, were worth more than

121
00:10:13,940 --> 00:10:14,557
companies.

122
00:10:16,626 --> 00:10:22,727
He watched it himself only once, in the back of the
car on the way to the airport, on

123
00:10:22,747 --> 00:10:24,095
Tomás's insistence.

124
00:10:24,666 --> 00:10:30,837
The Wesker half of the mind watched the eleven seconds
the way a surgeon watches a recording

125
00:10:30,857 --> 00:10:36,826
of his own operation: clinically, noting the timing,
the light, the angle. It had worked.

126
00:10:36,846 --> 00:10:39,990
He had known it would work. The
rest was noise.

127
00:10:40,559 --> 00:10:45,927
The other half was not watching the eleven seconds
at all. It was watching the numbers

128
00:10:45,947 --> 00:10:46,929
underneath them.

129
00:10:48,620 --> 00:10:50,200
Fifteen million.

130
00:10:50,720 --> 00:10:56,032
Twenty million views. Not eight million people.
Some of them watched it twenty times.

131
00:10:56,552 --> 00:11:02,867
I spoke to three hundred thousand people in a
stadium in Nuremberg. It was the largest

132
00:11:02,887 --> 00:11:08,393
crowd of my life. It took a year of planning
and a special train timetable.

133
00:11:09,594 --> 00:11:15,326
This morning you sat in a chair and said nothing,
and sixteen million people came.

134
00:11:17,346 --> 00:11:23,213
Wesker did not answer. For a long time there was only
the hum of the tyres and the lights

135
00:11:23,233 --> 00:11:25,859
of the highway sliding across the glass.

136
00:11:26,428 --> 00:11:32,054
Then the old voice spoke again, very quietly, and
there was something in it that Wesker

137
00:11:32,074 --> 00:11:34,715
had never heard before, and did not like.

138
00:11:35,404 --> 00:11:41,677
I have misjudged this world. I thought it was
soft. It is not soft. It is waiting.

139
00:11:43,697 --> 00:11:49,525
In March, Umbrella Life Sciences rang the opening
bell at the NASDAQ in Times Square,

140
00:11:49,545 --> 00:11:55,580
on the morning of a secondary share offering that
raised three point two billion dollars

141
00:11:55,600 --> 00:11:56,680
in a single day.

142
00:11:57,250 --> 00:12:03,144
He rang it with Catherine on one side of him and
Evelyn on the other, and Dana, and Tomás,

143
00:12:03,164 --> 00:12:08,729
and even Walter, who had been talked into a suit.
On the giant screens outside, seven

144
00:12:08,749 --> 00:12:14,577
storeys high above the traffic, the red-and-white
emblem turned slowly over Times Square,

145
00:12:14,597 --> 00:12:20,228
and a crowd on the pavement — who had come because
they had seen the eleven seconds on

146
00:12:20,248 --> 00:12:23,514
their phones — held up their
phones and filmed it.

147
00:12:24,083 --> 00:12:29,253
One of them had printed a T-shirt with a pair of
black sunglasses on it, and the number

148
00:12:29,273 --> 00:12:29,789
thirteen.

149
00:12:32,110 --> 00:12:35,653
Assessment. Thirty-seven thousand Points.

150
00:12:42,085 --> 00:12:44,360
Level up. Level twelve.

151
00:12:46,524 --> 00:12:51,404
Public fascination. The System had never
paid him for strangers before.

152
00:12:51,975 --> 00:12:57,557
The Adolf half of the mind read that line, and read
it again, and understood something

153
00:12:57,577 --> 00:13:03,094
that it would take the Wesker half another three
years to accept. The crowds had come

154
00:13:03,114 --> 00:13:05,961
back. They had simply moved
into the phones.

155
00:13:08,029 --> 00:13:13,597
In a one-bedroom apartment in Wilmington, Hannah
Price watched the eleven seconds for

156
00:13:13,617 --> 00:13:16,884
the forty-first time, with a
notebook on her knee.

157
00:13:17,455 --> 00:13:22,914
She was not watching his face. Everybody else was
watching his face. She was watching

158
00:13:22,934 --> 00:13:28,716
Miriam Castell's — the anchor's — as the silence went
on, and she was watching the precise

159
00:13:28,736 --> 00:13:34,261
moment, at about the seventh second, when the most
experienced interviewer in American

160
00:13:34,281 --> 00:13:39,870
television had stopped being in control of her own
programme, and had not even noticed.

161
00:13:40,440 --> 00:13:43,513
She wrote two words in the notebook,
and circled them.

162
00:13:44,082 --> 00:13:45,419
Eleven seconds.

163
00:13:45,990 --> 00:13:52,046
Then, underneath, she wrote the first line of a pitch
to a national magazine, for a long

164
00:13:52,066 --> 00:13:55,084
profile of a man nobody knew
anything about.
